WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.630
Breaking up the Yemeni state is better than
fighting to keep it together, right?

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:12.230
It’s an appealing thought and not unprecedented.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:16.340
Yemen used to be two countries—the North
and South united in 1990.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:19.570
Southerners started and lost a civil war in
1994, and

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.349
a secessionist movement re-emerged in 2007-2008.

00:00:21.349 --> 00:00:26.669
And the frontline of today’s war is largely
along the old North-South border.

00:00:26.669 --> 00:00:28.920
Re-dividing Yemen won’t bring peace.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.750
Or remove Iran’s and al Qaeda’s footprints.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:36.000
In the north, there will be a contest between
the former Yemeni president Ali Abdullah Saleh,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.180
and the Iranian-backed al Houthi movement.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.430
Regardless of who wins, the al Houthis will
keep a statelet in the north, preserving Iran’s

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:43.430
influence.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:47.050
Who provides much-needed financial support
to the north remains an open question.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.640
Saudi Arabia won’t and Iran can’t.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.550
The other half of the country—the South—is
far from stable.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:58.620
An ongoing power contest among various factions,
including the internationally recognized Yemeni

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.800
government, will distract from any concerted
effort against al Qaeda.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.220
Instead of solving Yemen, breaking up the
state just replicates the problems.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.960
What do you think the future of Yemen looks like?

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.540
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.600
Also let us know what other topics you want our scholars to cover in 60 Seconds,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.700
and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

